The Agent checks its private op/config/control and exact pending unused code
after its last fresh actor/role read, then returns the held code without
another await. The Registry receives that code, awaits public identity,
awaits fresh membership roles, checks only its own control/map/native rows,
and returns the held Agent result.

A concrete permitted interleaving is:

1. Agent grant completes its private candidate guard and hands code C to the
   trusted Registry. The Agent control records the nonsecret receipt.
2. Registry obtains the matching six-field public identity and begins its
   final role read. Hold completion within the existing five-second limit.
3. Another allowed Agent operation consumes C, or changes private config
   without changing the six public identity fields. Registry rows stay exact.
4. Return otherwise valid role facts and release the Registry turn before
   its signed deadline. Its local transaction still accepts and relays C.

This shows delayed delivery after the Agent candidate changed. It does not
show an unauthorized recipient, a usable revoked credential, an authority
bypass, a second mint, or a successful replay. An Agent mutation after its
trusted handoff resembles a mutation during ordinary delayed network delivery.
Any actual code/token use still owes fresh admission and exact candidate checks.

The contracts leave the response-owner boundary ambiguous. The accepted
Registry proposal assigns private candidate/config checks to the Agent and
explicitly disclaims a remote cross-object transaction. The role contract
linearizes admission at a fresh membership read. The accepted Issuer ordering
contract also says every new plaintext result gets an exact candidate check
immediately before return and rejects candidate mutation during a held final
read. That stronger local rule already applies to the Agent adapter, whose
candidate rows it owns. Registry cannot perform that same synchronous check
on remote Agent SQL using its public identity RPC.

## Requested planner ruling

For S4 Registry existing-grant and creation relay, does private candidate
release linearize at the trusted Agent handoff, followed by Registry's fresh
membership judgment and exact local control/map/native settlement? If so,
explicitly qualify that Registry may deliver a credential that changed or
expired after Agent release; it promises neither current remote candidate
validity at Registry delivery nor a lease across the later waits. Private
Agent checks and fresh checks on each actual credential use remain mandatory.

Or must Registry's later outward return satisfy an additional remote
candidate/owner validity protocol? If that stronger rule is required, specify
the bounded protocol and its linearization/expiry meaning before source
changes. Re-reading remote identity/candidate and then awaiting roles cannot
atomically lock both objects. Do not widen the six-field public identity,
add public proof/epoch fields, remint a secret, or claim cross-object atomicity
without a separate reviewed design.

Either ruling must preserve historical nonsecret receipts, one-shot
consumption, same-secret reuse effects, newer legitimate candidate protection,
local Registry ownership, existing absolute deadlines, and Native judgment.

